The snippet below puts the bug and three safe alternatives side by side so the difference is concrete. Drop it into a Java 17 console app — the first loop crashes, the rest don't.

```java
public class BoundsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] scores = {90, 85, 78, 92, 88}; // 5 elements: indices 0 to 4

        // WRONG — i <= scores.length means i goes 0,1,2,3,4,5
        // When i=5: scores[5] does not exist — exception thrown
        for (int i = 0; i <= scores.length; i++) {  // BUG: <= should be <
            System.out.println(scores[i]);
        }

        // CORRECT — i < scores.length means i stops at 4
        for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(scores[i]); // safe: 90, 85, 78, 92, 88
        }

        // SAFEST for read-only — enhanced for loop, no index needed
        for (int score : scores) {
            System.out.println(score); // compiler guarantees no out-of-bounds
        }

        // Direct access — always check before using a variable index
        int index = 10;
        if (index >= 0 && index < scores.length) {
            System.out.println(scores[index]); // safe
        }
    }
}
```

The takeaway: the strict `<` loop and the enhanced for loop are both safe, but the enhanced for is the one you literally cannot get wrong, because there is no index to manage.
